.TH QFW-SQUEUE 1 "September 2026" "qfw-slurm 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qfw-squeue \- show Slurm jobs with sanitized QPM allocation state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qfw-squeue
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qfw-squeue
connects to the QFw site directory as a DEFw client, reads sanitized scheduler
allocation summaries from registered QPM services, and correlates them with
.BR squeue (1)
JSON output.
Ordinary and heterogeneous components are grouped by their canonical Slurm
allocation identity.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-job " job-id"
Show one canonical Slurm allocation.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " username"
Show allocations owned by one user.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write a versioned JSON object instead of the terminal table.
.TP
.BI \-\-site-config " path"
Use the named QFw site configuration.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " seconds"
Bound each required data-source operation.  The default is 10 seconds.
.SH QUANTUM STATES
EVALUATING identifies a pending Slurm job without an accepted QPM reservation.
ACCEPTED identifies a pending job with a QPM reservation.  ACTIVE identifies a
running job with one or more live QPM reservations.  RELEASING identifies a
completing job whose QPM reservation is still visible.  UNAVAILABLE identifies
an active job without matching live QPM telemetry.
.PP
Released and rejected jobs are absent from the live Slurm queue or the live QPM
reservation store.  Historical terminal status belongs in a future accounting
view rather than this command.
.SH SECURITY
The command uses the QPM sanitized telemetry interface.  It does not display
reservation identifiers, application authorization material, or provider
credentials.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates complete output.  One indicates partial output caused by a
failed required data source.  Two indicates invalid command syntax.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
qfw-squeue
qfw-squeue --user user-a
qfw-squeue --job 42 --json
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR squeue (1),
.BR qfw-sinfo (1),
.BR qfw-slurm (7)
